  "world": {
   "notes": "Robuchon au Dôme is in the dome on the 43rd floor of SJM's Grand Lisboa hotel in Macau, under a chandelier of more than 131,500 Swarovski crystals. It has held three Michelin stars every year from 2009 to 2026, five stars from Forbes Travel Guide and three diamonds from the Black Pearl guide, and has won Wine Spectator's Grand Award every year since 2005 for a list of more than 17,000 labels, the largest in Asia. About 70 seats; lunch 12:00-2:30 and dinner 6:30-10:30, closed Tuesday and Wednesday; the August 2026 menus: at lunch, Menu Découverte MOP 998, Plaisir 1,398 and Gourmet 1,898, with a sommelier's selection of two, three or four 90ml glasses at MOP 588, 688 and 888; at dinner, the summer Menu de Saison (L'Été) at MOP 3,388 and the Menu Signature of Robuchon's emblematic dishes at MOP 3,888, both for the whole table only, and an à la carte from MOP 288 (cheese, desserts) to MOP 3,488 (Bresse chicken for two); all subject to a 10% service charge. Breads are baked in house. The wine-list items and the trophy bottles are real entries from the August 2026 list at its MOP prices. The wine list's foreword says wines are priced slightly above cost. The executive chef is Julien Tongourian; Joël Robuchon, who opened it, died in 2018. Most restaurants in Macau add a 10% service charge; restaurants have been exempt from Macau's 5% tourism tax since 2002; the pataca is pegged to the Hong Kong dollar at about 8.08 to the US dollar. The single tasting price here is the Menu de Saison at MOP 3,388 and its courses are that menu; lunch guests really pay less. The pairing price is the four-glass lunch selection, since the dinner pairing price isn't published. The menu, prices, hours and chef are from the restaurant's own published menus and page (August 2026); staff names below the executive chef, revenue, rent and the local events are estimates or invented.",
